welcome to the Macmillan report I'm  Marilyn Wilkes your host and our guest  is David Jackson a professor of  Portuguese at Yale University his  research interests include Portuguese  and Brazilian literature's commotions  machado de assis  fernando pessoa modernist vanguard  estándar arts literature Portuguese  culture in Asia and ethnomusicology  today we'll talk with professor Jackson  about his new book entitled machado de  assis a literary life  welcome professor Jackson thank you very  much well let's start by having you tell  us who this man was who is machado  machado de assis  is Brazil's greatest writer and all of  Latin America's greatest writer in the  end of the 19th the beginning of the  20th century he had a very long literary  life a formative period of some 25 years  where he wrote a journalism translated  plays was very active and resonated and  in the last thirty years where he  produced the novels for which and short  stories for which he's most known he was  a man of letters in every sense he wrote  in practically every genre novel short  story play journalism he did  translations of Victor Hugo of  Shakespeare and he became the preeminent  man of letters of his time in Brazil and  his novels that are considered now some  of the great novels of the end of the  19th century okay and tell us about your  book and what your goal was in writing  it so the goal of my book was to promote  Machado and present Machado as a great  writer of his time in the company of the  other European and world prose writers  of the end of the 19th century and what  were some of his contemporaries so he's  mentioned along with Henry James with  Proust with dust of yesica Gogol and of  course in the Burin Peninsula with  Esther de chaos and some of the other  prose writers he had translated Dickens  so he really fits in to the great  realist writers at the end of the  nineteenth century except that he  changed that whole tradition and he can  be seen and one of the objects of my  book is to present him as in a way the  inventor of the modernist novel and my  prose okay  very interesting so this he did by  changing a receive tradition he was a  great reader he read everything and his  works incorporate shall I say traces of  everything that he read but there  changed to be applied to a Brazilian  situation and also put across with his  characteristic humor and irony and his  narrative stance now I know you're a big  fan and I am curious what specifically  was it about  Machado that drew you to him I think  he's the first author I read when I  started studying Portuguese and the  fascination stayed with me and of course  I've taken a number of seminars and  continue to read Machado over the years  but his novels are so ingenious in a way  that they're sort of prime material that  people who do Portuguese study the  acquaintance with Machado sees through  translation actually had an early phase  in the 1950s and 60s a classical scholar  at UCLA named Helen Caldwell translated  three of his novels in a number of short  stories and did two books of  interpretation published in 1960 and  1970 William Grossman another translator  did the posthumous memoirs of bras Kubis  which is an early novel and a early  edition and these were to some extent  widely read at the time I had a Yale  alumnus who returned to take a course  with me after he had graduated and he  had read these translations in the 60s  and thought it was so unusual to have a  course available on Michoud JC's that he  came to read them again now we've come  to another cycle there are new  translations of a lot of the major  novels and the oxford latin american  library and there's been quite a lot of  renewed attention to Michelle Jesse's I  think the prominence of Brazil and  recent news has helped to promote that  and also there are a number of online  sites where you can access the works of  the complete works of Machado  and studies of him as well so that now  there's a new attention to mahadji Aziz  and our own Harold Bloom of course as  mentioned Machado is one of the great  authors of the Western tradition and  part of the objective of my book also is  to promote the incorporation of Machado  into the courses of comparative  literature and into the Canon let's say  of the world's major authors so if we  had to pick one of his works for  instance a novel perhaps give us a bit  of detail about it and overview so yes  he wrote four early novels in the 1870s  that are considered romantic but they  actually do have many characteristics of  what's called his mature fiction and  that consists of five novels starting in  1880 with the posthumous memoirs of bras  Kubus which has the interesting conceit  that the author has written the book  after death right then there's a an  interesting discussion among readers of  Machado whether that's his greatest  novel or whether it's dongha's Muro  which is a novel he wrote in 1900 which  is the retrospective memoirs of another  character so those two are generally  considered his greatest novels he also  wrote Kinkos bored whaha novel in 1890  was sort of a continuation of the first  and two final novels written in the 20th  century by a character named conselheiro  ideas  the interesting thing about all these  novels which is also fascinating for  readers is that they're not written by  machado de assis they're written by the  main characters who are their narratives  of their own memoirs and generally  written either retrospectively at a  point in old age or in the first one  after death so we have brass Kubus who's  dead and writing his memoirs we have  been to Santiago who's an old man  recalling the incidents of his earlier  life and justifying them and we have  councilor ideas who's a retired diplomat  who comes back to Rio after a long  career to face death but to interact  with the city of his memory mm-hmm okay  very interesting so Machado has a very  large body of work and also many have  written about him as well how did you go  about doing the research for your book  so the bibliography on Machado  of course mostly in Portuguese is  immense there's a 1955 work of  bibliography on Machado which is an  enormous volume so fortunately the you  know library is pretty good so I was  able to find a number of crucial  interpretations in Machado but that was  the question how to approach Machado now  in view of all of the books written  about him over time really from the 30s  to the present so actually I found  several accounts to be very useful one  of a curious Brazilian scholar named  Agra peda greco who knew all of Machado  sources these are this is a time when  people read everything there's no TV so  he says so this is from here and there  and even makes a curious comment when he  says I wish I had read Machado when I  was much younger because then I wouldn't  know on any of this and it would all  seem fresh and direct so that was a help  to me in showing how Machado had pieced  together sources from everything that he  read from different literature's  classical sources history and so forth  to compile his diverse narratives and  his style so earlier you had mentioned  that he's becoming more popular up until  this point I think he's been relatively  unknown even though there is such a huge  volume of writing about him why do you  think it is is his works translated into  English for instance so that's an  interesting question because after Helen  Caldwell there were a number of American  critics who discovered machado de assis  susan sontag wrote a long essay in the  new yorker that became very well known  Michael wood at Princeton John Barth the  author mentioned a lot of Machado and  Woody Allen write comments on machado de  assis  so it's not that he's unknown but I  would say that he was a lesser read and  when these translations came out they  wouldn't necessarily stay in print and I  think part of the problem and this is  pointing out is that there wasn't enough  criticism there was enough writing about  Machado that was available for people to  know more about him rather than other  than just the translations that were on  the market so I think we still need  books about machado de assis and  interpretations of Machado as a way of  helping students and other readers  to be able to fully appreciate the works  that are now in translation before them  alright and in his writing he employed a  number of key principles let's talk a  little bit about that so one of the  things that's most modern about his  novels is its narrative strategy and is  talking to the reader so we assume most  of his readers at the time were female  readers in Rio and he University  dialogues with them so well there was a  very small literate class at the time  and this was the Brazilian Empire and so  there wasn't that amount of literature  in Brazil at the time so he's very aware  that his readers maybe of course female  readers at home from this class and he  teases them plays with them to a large  extent and will say oh as my reader  knows and then he'll say the reader who  remembers chapter two will know that you  know so you have to go back to chapter  two and at one point Roz Kubus accuses  the reader and says the greatest defect  of my book is you reader right you're  the one who's keeping my book from being  everything it should be so the dialogue  with the reader is very important and it  makes the reader than part of the  process of the actual construction our  reading of the book and that's one of  his most innovative narrative features  and the other is using small chapters  some of which are put together only with  graphic signs so he's quite experimental  and inventive to that extent there's one  chapter that's just an epitaph on a  tombstone so in other words he's one of  the first authors to use a kind of what  shall I say Multimedia different  narrative structure semiotic approach to  putting the the novel together okay and  then conclusions what you conclude in  the book what I conclude in the book  Machado worked a lot with theater in his  early life he was very impressed with  the great actresses and actors who came  to Brazil there were Italian  Shakespearean actors who came in the  1870s there were great Sopranos who sang  with the operas mostly Italian that were  that were produced in Brazil and really  quite soon after their premieres in  Italy and actually one of the points I  make is that  the great heroines and Machado's novels  he created great female characters are  based to a certain extent probably on  these famous Sopranos whom he saw and  wrote about in Brazil but to a certain  extent he constructs in his novel a  theater of the world which is the city  of Regina - of his time the world of the  Empire as if it were to a certain extent  an opera on stage right so that opera  techniques of wretched Atif of dialog of  scenes and so forth really I think  helped to form and sustain his literary  works  so my last conclusion or my chapter is  called Machado and the spectacle of the  world which is the idea of the author  who is the observer of the very  theatrical performance of the world of  real generator that he constructs and  like another a number of other famous  authors he never left the city of  regenitive practically so the city was  to a certain extent his universe that he  knew very well and if one wanted to get  their feet wet for the first time we  with Machado what would you suggest they  read so there have been a number of new  translations of his short stories he was  a master of the short story from and so  the translations in English start with  the devil's Church the chapter of hats  there are a number of new volumes of  short stories that would be a very good  place to start are some of the later  trends the latest translations of his  novels I would say the posthumous  memoirs of bras Cuba's are Donkers  moo-hoo very famous works and open to  different kinds of interpretations so  the reader could jump right in and then  try to figure out what's going on very  exciting David thank you so much for  being here with us thank you for the  invitation some of your work very good  for more information about Professor  Jackson and his research please visit  our website at Yale that edu slash  macmillan report be sure to join us  again for another episode of the  Macmillan report made possible through  funding from the Whitney and Betty  Macmillan Centre for International and  area studies at Yale

